\section{Introduction}

%Despite numerous specialized applications and document management systems, file repositories are still widely used technical environments that are often worthy of preservation. The challenge from an archival perspective lies in the largely flexible handling of file repositories by single users and whole organizations, which often results in an unstructured way of storing these files, inconsistent file naming, redundancies, no or incomplete metadata, etc. As there is no special procedure to follow, the context of a file creation is often lost \cite{wendt2017,naumann2017kreative}. Furthermore, file repositories do not impose quantitative or qualitative limitations on the documents to be stored, so there is no restriction to the form the relevant information is stored in \cite{gillner2023abfragen}. Due to the almost unlimited amount of data and the fundamentally different mediality, the archival methods of appraisal, arrangement, and description in their current form are only applicable to a limited extent or require methodological and technical supplementation. Individual pilot projects of processing file repositories, largely manual and intellectual, support the assumption that the associated effort is insurmountable given the volume of records. Given the challenges mentioned and the volume of files to be processed, issues such as automatic extraction of metadata about the files are important research topics.
%remain as promising future research directions.

