%The ground truth has been manually created by professional archivists of the Friedrich-Ebert-Stiftung and includes a full set of ISAD(G) metadata for each of the documents. We describe in Section~\ref{sec:selectedISADG} the selected areas and elements within the ISAD(G) metadata as used in our evaluation, and in Section~\ref{sec:analysis} the evaluation results of comparing the results of various LLMs with the ground truth.
The ground truth was manually created by professional archivists of the AdsD and includes a full set of ISAD(G) metadata for archival materials in the dataset.  ISAD(G) stands for General International Standard Archival Description, a framework developed by the International Council on Archives (ICA) to standardize the description of archival materials. 
%It provides guidelines for creating consistent, multi-level descriptions of archives, ensuring that key information is captured in a structured way. 
It is widely used by archivists globally to make archival records more accessible and understandable. 
ISAD(G) organizes metadata elements into several areas, and each element serves a specific purpose in creating a comprehensive and standardized archival description. These elements can be divided into two groups: one group of elements whose values can be determined directly or by inferring from the archive material itself; another group of elements whose values need information from external sources or are assigned by an archivist. As a result, the elements in the second group are not suitable for evaluation.  We list the areas and elements of ISAD(G) considered in our evaluation in Table~\ref{tab:ISADG}, and also introduce their one-word tags,  which we use in the succeeding figures (Fig.~\ref{fig:totaleval} and \ref{fig:boxplots}) and tables (Table~\ref{tab:Improvements}).

%\subsection{ISAD(G) areas and elements considered in our evaluation}\label{sec:selectedISADG}

%Not all areas and elements specified in ISAD(G) are suitable for an evaluation when only the documents are available and without considering other context information. We list the areas and elements considered in our evaluation in Table~\ref{tab:ISADG}. We also introduce the abbreviations of the areas and elements, which we use in our succeeding figures (figures~\ref{fig:totaleval} and \ref{fig:boxplots}) and tables (Table~\ref{tab:Improvements}).

%We have excluded elements like the reference code in the identity statement area in the form of a unique identifier for the archival unit that is given by the archivist (typically according to some guidelines of its organization) for locating the material. Whole areas have been excluded from our evaluation because the information provided in these areas is typically not given in the document to be archived itself: Excluded areas include the context area that provides information about the origin and custody of the archival materials, the allied materials area that provides information about related materials or copies, the notes area that allows for additional information not covered elsewhere, and the description control area that documents the creation and maintenance of the archival description itself. In the content and structure area, we exclude the elements about appraisal, destruction, and scheduling information about decisions made regarding the selection, retention, or destruction of records, the accruals element indicating whether additional materials are expected to be added to the unit, and the element about the system of arrangement describing how the materials are organized, including any original order or imposed arrangement. In the conditions of access and use area, we have excluded elements about conditions governing access that specifies any restrictions on access, such as legal, privacy, or preservation concerns, the element conditions governing reproduction for details rules for copying or reproducing the materials, including copyright or institutional policies, and elements about finding aids that lists any tools or guides available to help users navigate the materials.


