\subsubsection{Action increment}
The one-step increment of virtual pitch rate reference is defined as
\begin{equation}
    \Delta q_{\mathrm{ref}(t+1)} = \left|q_{\mathrm{ref}(t+1)} - q_{\mathrm{ref}(t)}\right| 
\end{equation}\\
where $q_{\mathrm{ref}(t)}, q_{\mathrm{ref}(t+1)}$ are actor outputs in $t+1, t$ and are defined as

\begin{equation}
    q_{\mathrm{ref}(t+1)} = W^{\vartheta_{1}}(\hat{e}_{\alpha(t+1)},\hat{\alpha}_{t+1}, \hat{\delta}_{t+1} )
\end{equation}
\begin{equation}
    q_{\mathrm{ref}(t)} = W^{\vartheta_{1}}(e_{\alpha(t)},\alpha_{t}, \delta_{t}) 
\end{equation}

The one-step increment of control surface deflection is
\begin{equation}
    \Delta \delta_{t+1} = \left|\delta_{t+1} - \delta_{t} \right|
\end{equation}\\
where $\delta_{t+1}, \delta_{t}$ are actor network outputs in $t+1, t$ and are defined as:

\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
    &\delta_{t+1} = W^{\vartheta_{2}}(\hat{e}_{q(t+1)}, \hat{q}_{t+1}, \hat{\alpha}_{t+1})\\ 
    &\delta_{t} = W^{\vartheta_{2}}(e_{q(t)},q_{t},\alpha_{t}) 
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}

Figure \ref{action_incement_TS} compares the histories of action increments. The TS loss for the higher-level agent reduces the amplitude of $\vert\Delta q_{\mathrm{ref}(t+1)}\vert$ to less than 0.5 deg/s. For lower-level agent, the reduction of the amplitude of $\vert\Delta \delta(t)\vert$ is restricted by control surface deflection limits. The reason lies in that the vanilla control system does not penalize aggressive actions so that it learns a 'bang-bang' control strategy to reduce tracking errors, while the TS loss penalizes fast changes of actions so that a low-gain control law is learned.

