\subsubsection{One-step cost}
Figure \ref{reward1_comparison} compares one-step costs over time during online learning. For the higher-level agent, TS method provides small one-step costs in a starting phase [0-10]s. The one-step cost of the vanilla control system shows oscillations in a final learning phase [30-40]s because of the failure of angle of attack tracking (see Figure \ref{smoothness_q_comparison}). This failure is explained by a high-gain control law learned during continual learning. As a comparison, TS method avoids the agent learning a high-gain control law by constraining action increments over time. %The actor network is forced not to imitate a high-gain control law.  

For the lower-level agent, the oscillations of one-step costs in the vanilla control system are more obvious due to the unsuccessful tracking of pitch rate reference. The virtual control command $q_{\text{ref}}$ is a high-frequency oscillatory signal that brings difficulty into tracking (see Figure \ref{smoothness}). 

