\subsubsection{Activation function saturation level}
The saturation of an activation function $\tanh(\cdot)$ indicates its output approaches the maximum or minimum value due to large amplitude of input (see Figure \ref{saturation}). Meantime, its gradient vanishes and finally approaches zero. The vanishing gradient leads to slow-pace learning of control policy through gradient descent approaches. A worse effect is that a saturated activation function outputs maximum or minimum actions in response to tracking errors, resulting in a 'bang-bang' control effect. This 'bang-bang' control effect will degrade tracking control performance and system stability.

%es a 'slow-pace' learning to minimize the loss function by gradient-descent method. Additionally, the maximum or minimum actions from a saturated neuron in actor makes 'bang-bang' control effects, which degrades control performance.

%in control degrade control performance menon taken into the flight control system is the fast dynamics by aggressive actions taken by an over-saturated actor, providing a 'bang-bang' control from optimal control perspective \cite{Kamien1991}. 

The 'bang-bang' control strategy is usually learned through an overly aggressive learning process. Specifically, the reasons can be: (1) the RL agent uses a large learning rate. A large learning rate causes the actor’s weights to be updated with large increments, pushing the activation function into its saturation zone. (2) An inadequately tuned loss function. A loss function that underemphasizes the control effort can lead to comparable outcomes. In such cases, the actor learns to generate large control actions in response to tracking errors, without properly accounting for control effort. These large actions typically arise from saturated activation functions. Moreover, this aggressive learning behavior may persist even as the tracking error decreases, continuing to degrade control performance.

Figure \ref{saturation} shows the dynamical saturation level of the activation function in output layer of actors. The vanilla control system eventually makes $\tanh(\cdot)$ saturated in the range of input intervals [-4,-2], [2,4] (the higher-level agent), and input intervals [-3,-2], [2,3] (the lower-level agent). In these intervals, the derivative of $\tanh (\cdot)$ is less than 0.1, providing slight gradient for actor training. As a comparison, the TS-integrated control system avoids the saturation even starting with the same setting as the vanilla control system. The input of $\tanh(\cdot)$ is kept within 'unsaturated' intervals [-2,2] (the  higher-level agent) and [-0.5,0.5] (the lower-level agent). This demonstrates the effectiveness of a TS loss to avoid saturation of $\tanh(\cdot)$. 

