\subsubsection{Sensitivity analysis}

The sensitivity of the actor indicates how much the output will change, and in what direction, if the input slightly increases \cite{bib34}. High sensitivity may cause the actor to overreact to states variations and overcorrect the tracking error. This is also correlated to robustness of the actor against sensor noise and uncertainties.

The sensitivity measures for actors are defined as

\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
     K_{1(t)} &= \frac{\partial q_{\mathrm{ref}(t)}}{ \partial e_{\alpha(t)}}
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
    K_{2(t)} &= \frac{\partial \delta(t)}{ \partial e_{q(t)}}\\
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}\\
where $e_{\alpha(t)}=\alpha_{t} - \alpha_{\text{ref}(t)}, e_{q(t)} = q_{t}-q^{\prime}_{\text{ref}(t)}$ are tracking errors, $K_{1(t)},K_{2(t)}$ are sensitivity measures. 

Figure \ref{feedback_gain_TS} compares sensitivity measures. The vanilla control system shows oscillations of measures, indicating the actor is significantly sensitive to error variants. The TS-integrated control system shows reduced oscillations because the TS loss penalizes the increments of actions. The action increments are determined by actor sensitivity.

The relation between the virtual control and its sensitivity measure is

\begin{equation}\label{increment_q_ref}
\begin{aligned}
    &\vert q_{\text{ref}(t+1)}-q_{\text{ref}(t)} \vert \\
    \approx& \left| K_{1(t)}(e_{\alpha(t+1)}-e_{\alpha(t)})\right|\\ 
    =& \left| K_{1(t)}\right| \cdot \left|e_{\alpha(t+1)}-e_{\alpha(t)}\right|\\ 
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}

Similarly, the relation between control surface deflection and its sensitivity measure is

\begin{equation}\label{increment_delta_ref}
\begin{aligned}
    &\vert \delta_{(t+1)}-\delta_{(t)} \vert \\
    \approx& \left| K_{2(t)}(e_{q(t+1)}-e_{q(t)})\right|\\ 
    =& \left| K_{2(t)}\right| \cdot \left|e_{q(t+1)}-e_{q(t)}\right|\\ 
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}

%\begin{equation}\label{increment_delta}
%\begin{aligned}
%    &\vert \delta_{t+1}-\delta_{t} \vert \\
%    =& \left| K_{2(t+1)}e_{q(t+1)}-K_{2(t)}e_{q(t)}\right|\\ =& \left| K_{2(t)}e_{q(t+1)}-K_{2(t)}e_{q(t)}+(K_{2(t+1)}-K_{2(t)})e_{q(t+1)})\right|\\
%    =& \left| K_{2(t)}(e_{q(t+1)}-e_{q(t)})+(K_{2(t+1)}-K_{2(t)})e_{q(t+1)})\right|
%\end{aligned}
%\end{equation}


Equations \ref{increment_q_ref}, \ref{increment_delta_ref} show that the increments of (virtual) action can be reduced through reducing sensitivity. This is demonstrated in Figure \ref{feedback_gain_TS}.


