\subsection{Betweenness Centrality}

Betweenness centrality for a given node is the amount of time the node is present on the shortest path between any other two nodes.\cite{CURADO2022126560}

Mathematically, given G = (V, E, W), an undirected graph, where V is the set of nodes, E is set of edges and W is the weight of the node. 

Betweenness centrality is given as: \begin{equation}
C_t = \sum_{i \ne t} \sum_{\substack{j \ne i \\ j \ne t}} \frac{\sigma_{itj}}{\sigma_{ij}}.
\end{equation}
where: 
\begin{itemize}
  \item $\sigma_{ij}$ is the total number of shortest paths from node $i$ to node $j$,
  \item $\sigma_{itj}$ is the number of those shortest paths that pass through node $t$.
\end{itemize}


