\subsection{PASCAL: Energy Efficiency Results}
The intrinsic efficiency of SNNs can be attributed to their ability to perform sparse accumulates (AC), which is $\sim 5 \times$ more energy-efficient than the MAC operations performed by ANNs for 32-bit floating point representation~\citep{horowitz20141}. We estimate the energy efficiency of SNN over ANN by comparing the number of operations between the source ANN ($\#OP_{ANN}$) and the converted SNN ($\#OP_{SNN}$), as proposed by~\citeauthor{rathi2021diet} and formulated as
\begin{equation}
    \#OP_{SNN} = Spike Rate_l \times \#OP_{ANN}.
\end{equation}
$Spike Rate_l$ is the total spikes in layer $l$ over all timesteps averaged over the number of neurons in the layer. Table~\ref{table:ann_snn_energy} shows that SNNs inferred using PASCAL achieves equivalent accuracy as the source ANN while consuming up to $7\times$ lower energy. The energy results account for the fact that the first SNN layer alone performs MAC operations since it receives input image with real-valued pixel intensities. The formulae used to compute the ANN and SNN operations are detailed in Appendix~\ref{sec:MAC_comparisons}.


