\subsection{Adaptive Layerwise (AL) Activation Results}

Figure~\ref{fig:metric_values} shows the layerwise AL metric (refer Equation~\ref{metric}) for different models and datasets. We perform clustering with various values of $\chi$, and compute the quantization step per layer. We also evaluate the effective inference timesteps, $T_{eff}$, which is specified by 
\begin{equation}
    T_{eff} =  \frac{1}{|\mathbf{L_{tot}}|} \cdot \sum_{n=1}^{|\mathbf{L_{tot}}|} L_n
\end{equation}
where $\mathbf{L_{tot}}$ is the total number of layers. Table~\ref{tab:clustering_results} shows that adapting the quantization step layerwise leads to competitive accuracy using fewer effective timesteps, thereby improving the temporal efficiency. For instance, ResNet-18 provides 95.34\% accuracy on CIFAR-10 using $T_{eff}\approx2.4$, which is comparable to that (96.51\%) achieved using $8$ timesteps uniformly for all the layers.


\subsection {Comparison with the State-of-the-Art}

We compare our results (shown in Table~\ref{tab:sota_comparison}) with the QCFS framework~\citep{bu2023optimal} and the SEENN-1 method~\citep{li2023seenn}. Apart from these baselines, we also compare our work with various other state-of-the-art works~\ref{tab:othersota}. Our results show that PASCAL yields SNNs that provide competitive accuracy using fewer timesteps, consistently across models and datasets, compared to the QCFS framework~\citep{bu2023optimal}. We report comparable performance to the SEENN-1 method~\citep{li2023seenn} on CIFAR-10. However, we demonstrate significant performance improvements, both in terms of accuracy and effective timesteps, for CIFAR-100 and ImageNet. The efficacy of PASCAL improves with dataset complexity since it relies on mathematically equivalent ANN-SNN conversion.


\section{Conclusion}
In this paper, we proposed PASCAL, which is a precise and temporally efficient ANN-SNN conversion methodology. We showed that the proposed methodology enables ANNs to be converted into SNNs with a guarantee of mathematical equivalence, thereby leading to near-zero conversion loss. We formally proved that the converted SNN performs the same computations, unrolled over timesteps, as the source ANN with QCFS activation. We demonstrated the energy efficiency of the proposed approach using an analytical model. In addition, we also proposed an Adaptive Layerwise (AL) activation methodology to further improve the temporal efficiency during SNN inference. We presented a systematic and algorithmic approach to choose an optimal precision for each layer, which reduces the effective number of inference timesteps while providing competitive accuracy.


