\section{GPTKB Methodology}

The GPTKB methodology \cite{hu2024gptkbcomprehensivelymaterializingfactual} combines a recursive knowledge elicitation process with a post-hoc knowledge consolidation phase (see Figure~\ref{fig:methodology}).

\paragraph{Knowledge elicitation} \label{par:elicitation}
Starting from a seed subject, the LLM is prompted to return knowledge about it in the form of triples. New named entities in these triple objects are identified via LLM-based named-entity recognition (NER) and are enqueued for further elicitation in a recursive BFS-based graph exploration process. Constrained decoding is used to make sure that outputs stay within the triple format.
\paragraph{Knowledge consolidation} \label{par:consolidation}
To address the redundancy and variance introduced during knowledge elicitation, post-hoc knowledge consolidation is performed. In particular, we apply a greedy clustering algorithm to iteratively merge relations and classes into more frequent ones, given a sufficiently high label embedding similarity. 
% Second, we follow a standard blocking-based approach \cite{kopcke2010frameworks} to deduplicate instances of the class \textit{human}, choosing \textit{birth date} as blocking key. Finally, we propose an LLM-based taxonomy construction algorithm that initializes a high level taxonomy and iteratively incorporates classes (sorted by generality scores given by the LLM) from the KB.
\\[1em]
Further methodological details can be found in \citet{hu2024gptkbcomprehensivelymaterializingfactual}.
\section{GPTKB v1.5 Construction}

% Repeat Section 4 (implementation) from ACL paper
Although GPTKB v1.1 proved the viability of our methodology, the achieved output quality was below expectation. In particular, over 60\% of triples were estimated to be hallucinations, and significant problems occurred with output skew, with some entities having over 100k (virtually entirely hallucinated) triples.

For the present demo, we therefore decided to use a significantly stronger LLM. We opted for GPT-4.1, because it is among the strongest frontier models available as of July 3, 2025, and released less than 3 months ago.

Following the paradigm described in Section~\ref{par:elicitation}, We extracted knowledge from GPT-4.1 starting with the seed entity \textit{Vannevar Bush}. The whole process cost \$14,136 for OpenAI API calls and took 18 days. The final KB contains 100 million triples derived from 6.1 million entities in total, organized into 381k relations and 32k classes. 
We provide statistics of \ourkb{} in Table~\ref{tab:statistics}.

Since crawl parallelization distorts BFS search order, we then post-hoc recompute the shortest paths of each node to the root, and store this information in two meta-relations, \textit{bfsLayer} and \textit{bfsParent}, to enable structural insights. To facilitate data interchange, we also converted GPTKB into RDF format, and serialized it into Turtle syntax.

We performed two \textbf{quality evaluations}. An automated method based on web search, like in \citet{hu2024gptkbcomprehensivelymaterializingfactual}, using 1,000 random triples, and a manual assessment of 100 triples. Both annotations agree in the fraction of correct triples (75.5\% and 75\%), while the automated evaluation reported a slightly higher degree of incorrect ones (19.5\% versus 14\% in manual). In both cases, the truth of some triples remains undecidable, mostly, because parts of them are semantically incomprehensible.

