\section{GPTKB Web Provision}
% \todo{Talk about web server, SPARQL endpoint, RDF dump}
% \emptylines{15}
We provide multiple modes of access to GPTKB. 

\textit{Firstly}, GPTKB is hosted on the \url{https://gptkb.org} web server that provides a user interface to search entities via keyword queries and to perform link-based exploration to discover new connections and entities. Section \ref{subsec:demo-link-based-exploration} and Figure \ref{fig:browse} provide a demonstration experience of this link-based exploration. 
The web interface is implemented by using Python Django framework, and hosted on a Nginx web server.
The KB is stored in an OpenLink Virtuoso server.

\textit{Secondly}, we provide a SPARQL endpoint at \url{https://gptkb.org/query/} that supports structured queries within a timeout window of 100 seconds. 

\textit{Thirdly}, we provide the RDF dump under the CC BY 4.0 license on the HuggingFace datasets library at \url{https://huggingface.co/Knowledge-aware-AI}.



\section{Demonstration Experience}
We divide the demonstration experience in three parts: (1) link-based graph exploration, (2) structured SPARQL queries, (3) comparative analysis.

\subsection{Link-based Knowledge Graph Exploration}\label{subsec:demo-link-based-exploration}

Data about specific entities can be accessed via multiple routes: 
\begin{enumerate}
    \item The start page features a selection of direct links to entities such as \textit{Vannevar Bush} and \textit{San Francisco}.
    \item The web portal features a search field in the top-right corner, which can be used for string-based search.
    \item If an unambiguous entity name is known, one can directly access the entity's KB entry via the URL \url{https://gptkb.org/entity/<NAME>}.
\end{enumerate}
%
Figure \ref{fig:browse} shows how to initiate an entity search and continue with a link-based exploration. We start by typing this year's EMNLP venue, \textit{Suzhou}, which returns 227 results. Clicking on the first result takes us to its entity page. The entity \textit{Suzhou} contains 110 statements, and a user can click on any of the entity objects, here for instance, \textit{Suzhounese}, to further explore connected entities. 

Since each entity in GPTKB was identified as object from a parent entity during the BFS algorithm, we provide this information via the \textit{bfsParent} relation. Additionally, the \textit{bfsLayer} relation tells us at which layer knowledge elicitation was performed for the entity. In Figure \ref{fig:browse}, we learn that \textit{Suzhounese} is a child entity of multiple entities and that its triples were elicited in layer 4. Clicking on any of the parent entities lets a user move up the layers of GPTKB.

