\subsection{Joint Training}
\vspace{-3pt}


% \ours{} integrates representation learning for bug information retrieval and generative learning for bug correction into a unified model. We fine-tune a pre-trained large language model using both bug location and bug correction instruction data in a consistent format.

% % \zhenote{The definitions of M, i, and even $\mathcal{L}$ are not defined.}
% For bug location data, we employ a contrastive objective to compute the loss:
% \begin{equation}
% \mathcal{L}_{\text{Rep}} = - \frac{1}{M} \sum_{i=1}^M \log \frac{\exp(\tau \cdot \sigma(f_\theta(q^{(i)}), f_\theta(d^{(i)})))}{\sum_{j=1}^M \exp(\tau \cdot \sigma(f_\theta(q^{(i)}), f_\theta(d^{(j)})))}
% \end{equation}

% where \(\mathcal{L}_{\text{Rep}}\) denotes the contrastive loss function and \(f_\theta\) is our model parameterized by \(\theta\). The temperature hyperparameter \(\tau\) modulates the softmax distribution's smoothness, with higher values yielding softer distributions and promoting exploration. \(\sigma\) represents the pooling operation followed by cosine similarity computation. In this context, \(q\) and \(d\) represent query (instruction) and document (extracted information) samples, respectively. \(M\) denotes the batch size, while \(i\) indexes each data point and \(j\) indexes corresponding negative samples.


% For bug correction data, we utilize the language modeling objective:

% \begin{equation}
% \mathcal{L}_{\text{Gen}} = - \sum_{t=1}^T \log P_\pi(\hat{y}_t | \mathbf{x}, \hat{\mathbf{y}}_{<t})
% \end{equation}

% where \(P_\pi(\hat{y}_t \mid \mathbf{x}, \mathbf{\hat{y}}_{<t})\) denotes the probability of generating the \(t\)-th token \(\hat{y}_t\), which is the language modeling component used exclusively for generation. 

% We combine these objectives with loss weights \(\lambda\):

% \begin{equation}
% L = \mathcal{L}_{\text{Rep}} + \lambda\mathcal{L}_{\text{Gen}}
% \end{equation}

\ours{} integrates representation learning for bug information retrieval and generative learning for bug correction into a unified model. This approach fine-tunes a pre-trained large language model using both bug location and bug correction instruction data in a consistent format.

For buggy code location and bug type classification, \ours{} employs a contrastive objective to compute the loss, represented by the equation:

\footnotesize
\vspace{-3pt}
\[
\mathcal{L}_{\text{Rep}} = - \frac{1}{M} \sum_{i=1}^M \log \frac{\exp(\tau \cdot \sigma(f_\theta(q^{(i)}), f_\theta(d^{(i)})))}{\sum_{j=1}^M \exp(\tau \cdot \sigma(f_\theta(q^{(i)}), f_\theta(d^{(j)})))}
\]
\vspace{-3pt}
\normalsize 

It aims to maximize the similarity between matched query-document pairs while minimizing similarity with non-matching pairs. 
Here, $f_\theta$ represents the model parameterized by $\theta$, while $\tau$ is the temperature parameter controlling the distribution's softness. The symbol $\sigma$ denotes the pooling operation followed by cosine similarity computation. Query and document samples are represented by $q$ and $d$ respectively, with $M$ denoting the batch size. The index $i$ iterates over the batch, selecting query-document pairs $(q^{(i)}, d^{(i)})$, while the index $j$ in the denominator compares each query $q^{(i)}$ against all documents $d^{(j)}$, creating positive pairs when $i=j$ and negative pairs when $i \neq j$.

For bug correction, \ours{} uses the following objective:

\footnotesize
\[
\vspace{-3pt}
\mathcal{L}_{\text{Gen}} = - \sum_{t=1}^T \log P_\pi(\hat{y}_t | \mathbf{x}, \hat{\mathbf{y}}_{<t})
\vspace{-3pt}
\]
\normalsize 


This function maximizes the probability of generating the correct token sequence for bug correction.

The two objectives are combined with a weight $\lambda$:

\footnotesize
\[
\vspace{-3pt}
L = \mathcal{L}_{\text{Rep}} + \lambda\mathcal{L}_{\text{Gen}}
\vspace{-3pt}
\]
\normalsize 


This combined loss function enables \ours{} to simultaneously learn bug information retrieval and corrected code generation, capturing intricate relationships between bug patterns and their corrections within a shared parameter space.

