\subsection{Butterfly Gyroscope}%
\label{sec:butterfly}

The first example that we consider is the butterfly gyroscope from the Oberwolfach
Benchmark Collection~\cite{Bil05, morwiki_gyro}, which models a vibrating
mechanical gyroscope used for inertia navigation.
The model itself is a second-order system~\cref{eqn:soSys} with
$n = 17\,361$ states, $m = 1$ input and $p = 12$ position outputs.
The mass and stiffness matrices are symmetric, and the internal damping is
described by Rayleigh damping with $f(s) = \alpha = 0$ and
$g(s) = \beta = 10^{-6}$ according to~\cref{eqn:dampingModel}.
The response behavior of interest occurs in the high-frequency ranges.
We study this benchmark to illustrate how our proposed method applies to a
system with Rayleigh damping~\cref{eqn:Rayleigh}.

