%CPC is a emergent communication model for independent, decentralized agents originally proposed in the field of emergent communication research \cite{taniguchi2023emergent,hoang2024emergent,taniguchi2024collective}, as a multi-agent extension of predictive coding theory in computational neuroscience \cite{rao1999predictive,friston2006free}. In the field of developmental psychology, it is known that human communication beneficial to others tend to possess altruistic properties in natural environments \cite{tomasello1999cultural,tomasello2009we}. 
{\it Collective Predictive Coding} (CPC) is an emergent communication model for independent, decentralized agents, originally proposed in the field of emergent communication research \cite{taniguchi2023emergent,hoang2024emergent,taniguchi2024collective}. It extends predictive coding theory from computational neuroscience \cite{rao1999predictive,friston2006free} to multi-agent systems. In developmental psychology, human communication that benefits others is known to exhibit altruistic properties in natural environments \cite{tomasello2009we}.
%CPC assumes that human linguistic behavior and its acquisition are driven by an innately altruistic mechanism and is heavily inspired by the cognitive and motor development processes involved in human language learning \cite{taniguchi2024collective}. Rather than modeling emergence of communication through RL, CPC formulates it as inference within a single large generative model representing the group of agents. This model is then decomposed across individual agents, yielding an objective function that each agent can optimize through communication \cite{hagiwara2019symbol,hoang2024emergent,taniguchi2024collective}. 
CPC assumes that human linguistic behavior and its acquisition are driven by innately altruistic mechanisms, drawing inspiration from the cognitive and motor development processes involved in human language learning \cite{taniguchi2024collective}. 

Rather than modeling communication emergence through RL, CPC formulates it as inference within a single, large generative model representing a group of agents. This model is decomposed across individuals, yielding an objective function that each agent can optimize via communication \cite{hoang2024emergent,taniguchi2024collective}.
%This formulation enables decentralized Bayesian inference in a distributed manner \cite{hagiwara2019symbol,taniguchi2023emergent,hoang2024simsiam,nomura2025decentralized}. In this framework, messages in communication can be interpreted as auxiliary variables that support distributed optimization. Furthermore, interpreting multiple agents as a single generative model allows communication learning to be seen as a process of knowledge integration across agents based on individually acquired observation.
This formulation enables decentralized Bayesian inference in a distributed manner \cite{hagiwara2019symbol,taniguchi2023emergent,nomura2025decentralized}. In this context, messages are interpreted as auxiliary variables supporting distributed optimization. Viewing the multi-agent system as a single generative model also allows communication learning to be understood as a process of knowledge integration based on individually acquired observations.

%The application of CPC to MARL has previously been explored by Ebara et al. \cite{ebara2023multi} and Nakamura et al.\cite{nakamura2023control}, but their methods are based on posterior sampling using Markoc chain Monte Carlo (MCMC), which fundamentally limits the integration of neural network-based function approximation. In contrast, the present study proposes a CPC formulation grounded in variational inference, enabling implementation with deep neural networks and thereby offering broader applicability.
Previous applications of CPC to MARL have been explored by Ebara et al. \cite{ebara2023multi} and Nakamura et al. \cite{nakamura2023control}, but their approaches rely on posterior sampling via Markov Chain Monte Carlo (MCMC), limiting compatibility with neural network-based function approximation. In contrast, the present study introduces a CPC formulation grounded in variational inference, enabling implementation with deep neural networks and thus offering greater scalability and practical applicability.

