Our summarized ablation results clearly show the incremental performance improvement as each component is introduced. Furthermore, qualitative visualizations of segmentation maps illustrate specific improvements in boundary sharpness and intra-class consistency brought about by the combination of proposed modules. Through these experiments, we reinforce the rationale behind the design of SAIP-Net.

\section{Conclusion}
%In this paper, we presented SAIP-Net, a novel frequency-aware segmentation framework tailored for the challenges of remote sensing imagery. By introducing Spectral Adaptive Information Propagation, SAIP-Net effectively suppresses disruptive high-frequency noise within semantic regions and enhances boundary precision through adaptive frequency-domain filtering. Coupled with expanded receptive fields for improved contextual perception, our approach ensures more consistent feature representations and sharper object delineations across diverse scales and scene complexities. Extensive experiments on benchmark datasets demonstrate the clear advantages of our method over existing segmentation techniques, highlighting the effectiveness of integrating frequency-domain processing into the semantic segmentation pipeline for remote sensing applications.
%In this paper, we introduced SAIP-Net: Enhancing Remote Sensing Image Segmentation via Spectral Adaptive Information Propagation. By adaptively propagating spectral information and enlarging the receptive fields, our approach effectively eliminates disruptive high-frequency information within intra-class regions while preserving essential frequency-domain features along class boundaries. This results in improved intra-class consistency and enhanced boundary accuracy, thereby significantly improving the segmentation of complex structures in remote sensing images.

In this paper, we introduced SAIP-Net: Enhancing Remote Sensing Image Segmentation via \textbf{S}pectral \textbf{A}daptive \textbf{I}nformation \textbf{P}ropagation. By adaptively propagating spectral information and enlarging the receptive fields, our approach effectively eliminates disruptive high-frequency information within intra-class regions while preserving essential frequency-domain features along class boundaries. Experimental results confirm that our design significantly improves intra-class consistency and enhances boundary accuracy, thereby substantially improving the segmentation of complex structures in remote sensing images.




