\section{{Conclusion}}
\vspace{-0.05in}
We have presented NavEX, a novel dispatch coverage framework that provides a robust methodology for multi-agent deployment in non-convex and uneven environments. By combining exemplar-clustering with obstacle-aware and traversability-aware distance metrics, NavEX offers a unified approach to fair-access and hotspot deployment. The submodular optimization foundation of NavEX enables efficient, near-optimal solutions with provable performance guarantees. Simulations demonstrate the framework's effectiveness in complex, realistic settings. Future work will include extending NavEX to heterogeneous agents and integrating real-time adaptability to dynamic conditions like moving targets and changing environments, enhancing the practical relevance for diverse real-world applications.


%In this paper, we introduced NavEX, a multi-agent deployment framework tailored for coverage optimization in non-convex and uneven environments using exemplar-clustering. We define the deployment problem using a submodular maximization framework which offers the flexibility to define environment-aware distance as long as they remain a positive metric. We propose a utility function that handles both fair access and hotspot deployment scenarios while minimizing the agent-target distance and accounting for obstacles and varying traversability. We leverage two key distance metrics--Visibility Graphs for the exact shortest path in planar environments and traversability-aware RRT$^\star$ for navigable uneven terrains. Our approach provides a quantifiable worst-case performance bound of $(1 - \frac{1}{e})$, by leveraging the inherent submodular property of our k-medoid utility function while also ensuring a polynomial-time solution. We validated that NavEX positions the agent to achieve equitable pairwise distance minimization for fair access and prioritize densely populated targets for hotspot deployment, demonstrating improvement over distance metrics that neglect terrain constraints or obstacles. Future work will include extending NavEX to heterogeneous agents and integrating real-time adaptability to dynamic conditions like moving targets and changing environments enhancing the practical relevance for diverse real-world applications.  


