\subsection{Functional Validation}

\subsubsection{Test-case 1: Real-Time Trajectory Generation}
\label{sec:testcase1,traj}
One of the main use-cases of VISY-REVE is the real-time in-the-loop synthesis of images, only needing a sparse dataset as input. VISY-REVE can be used as a replacement for synthetic rendering or laboratory acquisition to close the loop for testing image processing algorithms. Combined with a GNC stack, VS therefore enables real-time \textbf{novel trajectory synthesis} \cite{wang2024freevsgenerativeviewsynthesis}. Note also that VS is even able to synthesize images that lie outside the convex hull of the dataset. For best results, however, the lighting position should remain the same or similar across the dataset. 

We demonstrate this use-case qualitatively: we synthesize a trajectory with small deviations from an existing one, see~\ref{fig:traj_mesh}. Pose estimation is done for both trajectories using the same CNN as before, combined with PnP. The errors are shown in~\ref{fig:pnp}. As can be seen, the poses can accurately be reconstructed from the synthesized images most of the time. 

