To restore the original scale, the output \( \widetilde{\kedit{\bm{y}}} \) is rescaled by multiplying it by the scale factors \( \gamma \) and \( \beta \), and dividing by the quantization range \( Q_p \). The rescaled output \( \kedit{\bm{y}} \) can be expressed as:
\begin{align}
\kedit{\bm{y}} = \frac{\widetilde{\kedit{\bm{y}}} \gamma  \beta }{Q_p}\keditcamera{.}
% \kedit{\bm{y}} = \widetilde{\kedit{\bm{y}}} \times \gamma \times \beta / Q_p
\end{align}
Following BitNet \kkkedit{b1.58~\cite{ma2024era}}, we do not use the bias. Due to the non-differentiable functions such as clipping and rounding, we apply the straight-through estimator~\cite{Bengio2013ste} during backpropagation to approximate the gradient.  %\hedit{as described in Sec.~\ref{sec:qat}}.
% スペースがないので削除
\if 0
\sedit{Specifically, we compute $\widetilde{\bm{W}}$ as follows:}
\begin{align}
\widetilde{\bm{W}} = \text{Detach}(\widetilde{\bm{W}} - \bm{W}) + \bm{W},
\end{align}
where $\text{Detach}$ denotes the detach operation, which prevents gradient calculation for it.
\fi


