To set the initial context for the research, the top three ranked passages are used based on the query entered by the user, in this case the content of the {\tt{question}} field for LiveRAG. Retrieval was done with an in-house index of the FineWeb-10BT collection. A ColBERT architecture search engine~\cite{ecir2022colbert-x} was used, which creates a dense vector per token for both the passage and query. We divided documents into non-overlapping passages of 450 tokens each for indexing. For efficiency, the index was compressed using the PLAID-X version\footnote{\url{https://pypi.org/project/PLAID-X/}} of ColBERT. Since it was possible for FineWeb-10BT to contain non-English documents, we chose to use an XLMR model\footnote{https://huggingface.co/FacebookAI/xlm-roberta-large}~\cite{conneau2019unsupervised} fine-tuned using translate distill~\cite{ecir2024translate-distill} for multilingual retrieval, which is trained with English queries and documents in several languages including English and Spanish. The training dataset is MS~MARCO~\cite{msmarco}. MS~MARCO documents were translated in-house with Sockeye Version~2~\cite{sockeye2} to create the training data.

The prompt in Figure~\ref{fig:research_prompt} was used to determine additional queries that were used for retrieval. We set fields referenced in the prompt as follows:
\begin{itemize}
\item {\tt{max\_iterations}} - 2
\item {\tt{task}} - the value of the {\tt{question}} field
\item {\tt{date}} - the date the software is run
\item {\tt{context}} - a concatenation of the top three ranked passages using the {\tt{question}} as the search query
\item {\tt{dynamic\_examples}} - based on {\tt{max\_iterations}} and in this case is "query 1", "query 2"
\end{itemize}
This prompt was issued to the Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct-Turbo model.  The top three passages were kept from each of the two queries generated as well as the original query. This created a list of nine passages. % depending on whether there was any overlap among the returned documents of the three queries.
Our PLAID-X search engine was used to return passages for each the queries.

The final step of conducting research utilized the m2-bert-80M-8k-retrieval model to embed both the original {\tt{question}} and the passage in chunks of 1000 characters with 100 character overlap, referred to as snippets herein. 
