\begin{abstract}
    %
    %
    %
    %
    %

    %
    \textbf{Background and Context.} Generative AI (GenAI) is becoming increasingly common in computer science education, yet its effects on student learning, skill development, and perceptions remain relatively underexplored. Despite widespread concerns about overreliance on GenAI, few studies have systematically examined how structured scaffolding can shift students’ engagement with GenAI tools in formal educational settings.

    \textbf{Objectives.} We investigate whether and how a dedicated ``AI-Lab'' intervention, which emphasizes scaffolding and mindful usage, shapes students’ understanding, perceptions, and applications of GenAI tools in undergraduate computer science and first-year engineering courses. Our main goal is to determine whether students develop a more deliberate approach to using GenAI that preserves and enhances essential skill building.

    \textbf{Method.} Over three regular semesters at Purdue University, we implemented the AI-Lab in two mandatory Data Structures and Algorithms courses, one elective Competitive Programming course, and one first-year engineering course, yielding 831 valid student responses. Using a mixed-methods approach, we collected and analyzed quantitative survey data (pre- and post-intervention) as well as qualitative focus group transcripts to capture both broad trends and in-depth perspectives.

    %
    \textbf{Findings.} While the overall frequency of GenAI usage for homework or programming projects remained largely stable, we observed large effect sizes in comfort and openness across conceptual, debugging, and homework problems. Notably, usage patterns for debugging also shifted statistically significantly, reflecting students’ more mindful and deliberate approach. Focus group discussions corroborated these results, suggesting that the intervention ``bridged the gap'' between naive GenAI usage and more nuanced, reflective integration of AI tools into coursework, ultimately heightening students' awareness of their own skill development.

    \textbf{Implications.} Our findings underscore that structured scaffolding -- such as the AI-Lab -- can help students harness GenAI’s benefits without undermining core problem-solving abilities. By demonstrating how student perceptions and practices evolve through targeted intervention, we provide evidence-based guidance for educators seeking to integrate GenAI effectively and responsibly into computing curricula, and we highlight directions for further research on GenAI-supported pedagogy.

\end{abstract}

