\section{Introduction}

The integration of AI into computer science education has been well documented, evolving alongside advancements in both technical and theoretical aspects of the field \cite{denny1011453624720, tlili2024can, sentance2022perspectives, donaire2025tiza}. However, the widespread availability of generative AI (GenAI) tools has significantly disrupted academic settings. In particular, large language model-based tools enable students to generate human-like text based on prompted instructions, raising new pedagogical and ethical challenges \cite{lau2023BanIt, Becker2023programmingIsHard, Finnie22, Moradi23}. The academic community responded quickly by examining the capabilities of these tools, assessing their impact on established educational practices, and exploring both the potential benefits and challenges associated with their use in learning environments \cite{Kerslake10114536415543701934, Hassan10114536415543701906, Raihan10114536415543701863}.

Existing guidance on integrating GenAI in education primarily adopts a broad academic perspective, focusing on classroom applications. Common strategies emphasize equipping students with the skills to critically evaluate the accuracy and efficacy of AI-generated content while fostering discussions on ethical considerations. Bull \textit{et al.} propose a pedagogical approach to computing education that introduces students to how professional software developers leverage GenAI in industry \cite{Bull23}. Their framework underscores the importance of cultivating problem-solving skills through scaffolding and fading techniques, iterative assignment modifications, and a structured, gradual immersion process.

To support AI literacy in computing education, the SEAME framework has been introduced, structuring lessons around five key dimensions: social impacts, ethical considerations, application of AI, mechanisms of AI functionality, and the evaluation of AI systems \cite{harrison}. Similarly, researchers at Aalto University in Finland have explored the use of GenAI to provide personalized learning experiences and automated feedback in computing education. Their approach emphasizes training students to write effective prompts and leveraging AI tools to improve programming skills \cite{Logacheva10114536326203671103}.

Several studies highlight the necessity of educating students on the ethical implications and limitations of GenAI \cite{ozkaya_2024, Generative_Ai_2025_scholarlyteacher}. Key concerns include safeguarding sensitive information, recognizing the potential inaccuracies of AI-generated content, understanding AI’s capacity to produce harmful material, and adhering to platform-specific terms of use.

In the spirit of protecting the integrity of CS education, we use the AI-Lab Intervention, first introduced by \cite{dickey2024innovating} in 2023 on the preprint server \url{arXiv.com} and published the following year, in second-year, collegiate computer science and first-year engineering courses and provide the first empirical evaluation of its effectiveness over the course of two semesters.  We study it using a mixed methods model with pre- and post-surveys as well as focus groups, and present our findings in \Cref{sec:discussion}.  Detailed breakdown of student population and courses can be found in \Cref{sec:results:student_populations}.


