\subsection{AI-Lab}

The AI-Lab framework \cite{dickey2024innovating} offers a structured approach for instructors to introduce GenAI to students in a pedagogically effective manner. Grounded in Vygotsky’s More Knowledgeable Other theory, this framework positions the instructor as an expert in the course’s technical content while acknowledging the evolving nature of GenAI tools. Rather than assuming absolute authority over AI applications, instructors are encouraged to engage with these tools in the classroom at the same level as students, providing guidance and scaffolding to facilitate meaningful learning experiences.

\begin{figure*}
    \centering
    \includegraphics[scale=0.5]{img/AI-Lab_Framework.png}
    \caption{AI-Lab Framework for integrating GenAI in programming courses \cite{Bejarano10114536415553705201}.}

    \label{fig:ailab}
    \Description{AI-Lab Framework aimed at faculty for integrating GenAI in programming courses.}
\end{figure*}

The AI-Lab framework is structured into multiple stages (see Figure \ref{fig:ailab} from \cite{Bejarano10114536415553705201}) to facilitate a guided exploration of GenAI in education. The first stage (Instructor Preparation) involves the instructor selecting an AI tool and a relevant topic, allowing them to assess the nature and accuracy of the tool’s outputs. Ideally, the chosen topic should expose AI limitations, producing incorrect or ambiguous responses to prompt critical analysis.

In the second stage (Prelab), students engage with offline preparatory materials designed to familiarize them with fundamental AI concepts, prompting strategies, and the generation of scholarly content. At this stage, students also complete an anonymous survey to report their prior usage and perceptions of AI tools in their learning process.

The third stage (In-class Lab) consists of an in-class intervention in which the instructor integrates AI into the lesson to generate responses to basic definitions, examples, and analytical scenarios. This demonstration is followed by a structured discussion on the appropriate and ethical use of AI in education. Students are then encouraged to experiment independently with the AI tool by posing more complex queries related to the course topics. Peer discussion is central to this process, as students share their findings, critique incorrect AI-generated outputs, and collaboratively propose corrections.

In the fourth and final stage (Post-Lab), students complete a homework assignment that requires them to apply AI tools to solve a set of problems. They must provide evidence of their attempts to guide the AI toward a correct solution or document instances where the AI failed to generate accurate results. The initial survey is administered again to measure shifts in students’ perceptions and engagement with AI, allowing for a comparative analysis of attitudinal changes throughout the intervention.


