\subsection{Statistical Analysis}

\subsubsection{Paired Sample Analysis}
A before-and-after study design was employed to evaluate the impact of the intervention on measured outcomes. The same participants were assessed at two time points: before (pre-test) and after (post-test) the intervention. To determine whether significant differences existed between pre- and post-intervention responses, a Paired Sample t-test was conducted.

The assumptions for the paired sample t-test include:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item The differences between paired samples follow a normal distribution.
    \item The samples are paired, meaning the same participants were measured under two different conditions.
\end{enumerate}
To assess the normality assumption, a Shapiro-Wilk test was performed on the distribution of paired differences. If the assumption was violated, a Paired Wilcoxon Signed-Rank Test was used as a non-parametric alternative, which evaluates the median difference rather than the mean difference.

\subsubsection{Effect Size Estimation}
%
%
To quantify the magnitude of observed differences, Rank-Biserial Correlation ($r_{rb}$) was used as the effect size measure. Rank-Biserial Correlation is the most appropriate effect size metric for paired data because it directly reflects the proportion of favorable and unfavorable differences within matched samples. Using the paired Wilcoxon test statistic, \textit{W}, it provides an interpretable measure of how strongly values increased or decreased between pre and post-intervention.

Effect size interpretation (Rank-Biserial Correlation metric $r_{rb}$):
\begin{itemize}
    %
    \item $\pm0.00 - \pm0.10 \rightarrow$ Negligible effect
    \item $\pm0.10 - \pm0.30 \rightarrow$ Small effect
    \item $\pm0.30 - \pm0.50 \rightarrow$ Medium effect
    \item $> \pm0.50 \rightarrow$ Large effect
\end{itemize}

\subsubsection{Significance Threshold}
All statistical analyses were conducted using two-tailed tests, with a significance threshold of p < 0.05 (95\% confidence level).



