\subsection{Focus Groups and Data Collection}\label{sec:methods:qual}
%

This study employs a mixed methods approach, integrating qualitative data to enrich insights into perceptions, strategies, and reflections of computer science and engineering students on GenAI usage. Qualitative data were drawn from focus group discussions post-intervention with questions that encouraged students to reflect on their use of AI before and after participating in the study. Focus groups allow for more flexible and in-depth data collection than individual assessments, encouraging natural participant interaction \cite{freitas1998focus}.%

Given the study's focus on capturing nuanced changes in student behavior and attitudes, we used qualitative methods to supplement survey data. This allowed for a more comprehensive understanding of how the AI-Lab intervention influenced students' learning experiences. Qualitative approaches help explore complex social processes, capture underlying motivations, and provide a deeper understanding of participants' perspectives, which are often difficult to measure quantitatively \cite{curry2009qualitative}.

On average, the focus groups occurred 5-7 weeks after the intervention at the end of the Fall semester, allowing students to experience the full scope of the intervention before reflecting during the focus group discussion. This approach gave students reasonable time to reflect on their AI Lab experience, capturing longer-term perceptions rather than just immediate responses. This enabled richer insights into retained learning and behavioral changes.

We provided equal opportunity for participants to join the focus group interviews. They were recruited by email with information about how to sign up, and there was a monetary incentive for participation. We scheduled the group interviews based on students' availability and conducted six sessions, each with a different number of participants.

Before data collection, participants signed consent forms. During the focus group interviews, we followed the protocol by informing students that their participation was voluntary and that they could leave at any time if they felt uncomfortable. We also clarified that they were not required to answer any questions they did not feel comfortable answering. Their names were anonymized to address ethical concerns and prevent conflicts of interest with two researchers from the study who hold a power dynamic over the participants. We collected their responses using audio recorder devices, and participants were informed that the recordings would be discarded after use. During the session, a researcher asked questions inciting the group's reflection on their understanding, experiences, challenges, and behavioral changes related to GenAI usage before and after the AI lab intervention. There was minimal intervention from the researcher during these sessions, as they acted more as an observer and facilitator rather than an active participant.

Together, these qualitative methods provided a comprehensive approach to exploring students' experiences, reflections, and behavioral changes related to GenAI usage, complementing the quantitative findings. Next, we describe the data analysis approach adopted in this study.


\subsection{Qualitative Analysis}

We transcribed and analyzed data from the focus group interviews using thematic analysis, which offers a flexible yet rigorous approach for identifying, analyzing, and interpreting patterns in qualitative data, particularly suitable for exploring participants perceptions and experiences \cite{braun2006thematic, saldana2013manual}.

We began the analysis using a deductive approach, capturing the language from the research questions to develop initial themes. This approach guided our early coding framework. After our initial glance at the data, we engaged in theme refinement through discussion, going back and forth between the data and the emerging codes. This occurred through an iterative process with theme development evolving through dialogue between two researchers and their interpretations. The researchers coded the same two transcripts first, comparing results and reaching agreement before moving on independently. This builds intercoder agreement and supports credibility in the findings. This also supports researcher triangulation, where multiple researchers provide diverse interpretations, increasing trustworthiness. Moving forward, we refined theme names and definitions and when we reached an agreement, we organized codes, applying them to transcripts using NVivo software. Please see \Cref{sec:results:qual_analysis} for this analysis and \Cref{app:focus_group_questionnaire} for the questionnaire used.

Our final set of themes captured broad trends and subtle tensions in the data. These included:

\begin{itemize}
    \item Exploration and Strategic Use
    \item Stable Frequency, usage evolvement
    \item Improved Understanding of AI's limitations
    \item AI as a Support Tool for Learning
    \item AI avoidance and boundaries
    \item Ethical Concerns and Internal Tension

\end{itemize}

The final themes served as the foundation for our qualitative findings presented in \Cref{sec:results:qual_analysis}. These findings illustrate how students engaged with GenAI tools and how their perceptions shifted after the intervention.



