\subsection{Student population}\label{sec:results:student_populations}
%

%
%
%
%
This intervention was performed at Purdue University in three computer science (CS) courses and one engineering (ENGR) course over Spring 2024 (S24) and Fall 2024 (F24).  Both CS and ENGR programs are highly competitive, rigorous programs. Not all courses were held every semester.  DSA-CS (CS 25100, \textit{Data Structures And Algorithms}, for CS majors) was held in both semesters and is a core course for CS majors held in their third or fourth semester (no earlier).  DSA-DSAI (CS 25300, \textit{Data Structures And Algorithms For DS/AI}) is the same course for Data Science and AI majors, more focused on interesting applications of data structures and less focused on what are commonly considered ``core'' data structures such as red-black trees (priority queues still covered, etc.), held in their fourth semester (no earlier).  CP (CS 21100, \textit{Competitive Programming I}) is an elective credit typically taken by second year CS students, not required by any degree plan.  ENGR (ENGR 13000, \textit{Transforming Ideas Into Innovations}) is a first-year engineering course covering skills in managing complex problems related to design, systems analysis with computational tools, and academic professional development.  ENGR is left out of the data unless explicitly noted otherwise, due to the primary research focus being on CS undergraduates.
%


%
We also analyzed the differences across demographics like gender, race/ethnicity, residency, and classification (sophomore/junior/senior) and found no significant differences between these groups.


