Each test begins with the assumption that a paired t-test is appropriate. To verify the normality assumption, the Shapiro-Wilk test is applied to the differences in paired observations. The results indicate that all differences have p-values less than 0.05, leading to the rejection of the null hypothesis that the data follows a normal distribution. Consequently, the data is considered non-normal.

Given this outcome, the Wilcoxon signed-rank test, a non-parametric alternative to the paired t-test, is employed. This test evaluates whether there is a statistically significant difference between paired responses without requiring the assumption of normality.

The hypotheses tested are as follows for the Wilcoxon signed-rank test:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Null Hypothesis (H$_0$): There is no difference between the paired responses.
    \item Alternative Hypothesis (H$_a$): A significant difference exists between the paired responses.
\end{itemize}

\Cref{tab:pvalues_combined_no_ENGR} below presents the results of the analysis. All but three questions exhibit statistically significant differences, indicating strong evidence to reject the null hypothesis for most of the questions.

Statistically significant questions:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item How \underline{open} are you to using GenAI to get help with homework problems?
    \item How \underline{open} are you to using GenAI to get help with debugging?
    \item How \underline{open} are you to using GenAI to get help with conceptual questions?
    \item How \underline{comfortable} are you using GenAI to get help with conceptual questions?
    \item How \underline{comfortable} are you using GenAI to get help with debugging?
    \item How \underline{comfortable} are you using GenAI to get help with homework problems?
    \item How \underline{frequently} do you use GenAI to get help with debugging?
\end{enumerate}

%
%
%
\subsubsection{Effect Size}

For statistically significant questions, we conduct post-hoc effect size analysis using Rank Biserial Correlation. This measure quantifies the magnitude of change between pre- and post-test responses, offering deeper insight into the intervention’s impact. Rank biserial correlation is particularly useful in paired data settings, as it evaluates differences on an individual level, showing how each student was affected by the intervention.

In this case, all statistically significant questions exhibit large effect sizes, indicating that the intervention led to substantial and meaningful changes in student responses. This reinforces the practical significance of the findings, demonstrating not only statistical significance but also consistent and impactful improvements across all measured areas. The ability to assess paired differences at an individual level further strengthens the interpretation, providing clear evidence of the intervention's effectiveness on a student-by-student basis.


