\subsection{Findings from Focus Groups Discussion}\label{sec:results:qual_analysis}
%

We analyzed focus group data from multiple courses during Spring 2024 to explore how students understood and applied GenAI tools before and after the AI Lab intervention. Below, we present the themes that emerged through iterative coding and reflection on student experiences. These findings are organized according to our research questions, combining recurring themes into a logical narrative. Still, we also acknowledge unique voices and even disagreements that enrich the analysis.  Please refer to \Cref{app:focus_group_questionnaire} for the focus group guiding questions, developed to answer the research questions presented in \Cref{sec:intro:RQs}.

%
%
%
\subsubsection{RQ1: How and how much are students using GenAI before and after the AI-Lab intervention?}

%
%
%
\paragraph{From Exploration to Strategic Use}%
Students reported they initially used GenAI in a trial-and-error manner and their prompts often lacked structure. Before the intervention, they mainly used such tools for summarization, research, and translation. Many struggled with unclear prompts and doubtful AI responses. This caused frustration and confusion, particularly due to the inaccuracy of AI tools. Thus, before the AI Lab, students lacked prompting skills and often abandoned AI when it didn't work immediately, as described by these participants:

\begin{quote}
    ``Usually whenever I try to use ChatGPT, I sort of just give up on it really fast if it doesn't give me the right answer.'' – Focus Group 5, Speaker 3

    ``Before we hadn’t really been given guidelines on how to use it… I thought it was helpful in guiding us, giving different examples of how it could be applied.'' – Focus Group 1, Speaker 3
\end{quote}

After intervention, students reported learning how to refine prompts, add context, and engage in more interactive, iterative conversations with GenAI tools. They began using more structured prompting techniques:

\begin{quote}
    ``Now I give it, for example, everything I do know… don’t change those answers. Based on what I have, give me the answer to A.'' – Focus Group 6, Speaker 2

    ``I felt like it did much better once it was given clear rules… especially if I would explicitly dictate what I wanted it to tell me.'' – Focus Group 5, Speaker 1
\end{quote}

These reflections illustrate that while the frequency of use may not have increased for all students, their approach shifted substantially from casual to more purposeful.

%
%
%
\paragraph{Frequency Remained Stable, Yet Rationale for Use Evolved}
While participants clearly demonstrated a significant shift in their approach, which shows the intervention improved prompting practices, it did not lead to a major change in how often students used GenAI. For instance, before the intervention, some students used AI frequently, while others rarely interacted with it. After the intervention, those students reported maintaining a similar level of use, although engaging with the tools more intentionally and effectively. As one student shared:

\begin{quote}
    ``It's pretty much the same. I just learned better ways to use it.'' – Focus Group 1, Speaker 3.
\end{quote}

Another student corroborated this point, emphasizing how their approach became more refined:

\begin{quote}
    ``I don’t think I use it more, but I definitely use it better. Now I know how to refine my prompts instead of wasting time rewriting them over and over.'' – Focus Group 3, Speaker 2
\end{quote}

That being said, a few students did report increased usage after the lab due to learning better ways to interact with the tool:

\begin{quote}
    ``I had used it sparsely... after that I found myself using it maybe twice a week for a variety of different classes.'' – Focus Group 5, Speaker 1
\end{quote}

In contrast, some students consciously reduced their use because of concern for becoming too dependent on AI. They mentioned being worried that overuse might impair their learning, critical thinking, or academic integrity. This concern emerged in a few responses, such as:

\begin{quote}
    ``At some point, I felt like I was relying too much on ChatGPT. I didn’t want it to replace my own thinking, so I started using it less.'' – Focus Group 4, Speaker 3
\end{quote}

%
%
%
\paragraph{Improved Understanding of AI's Limitations}
Students became more skeptical and discerning after the AI Lab. They learned that AI tools sometimes present false information and generate fictional data. This awareness led participants to more cautious and critical usage behaviors post-intervention. At the same time, students refined their use, and they also became more aware of GenAI's limitations, especially its tendency to sound confident even when it's wrong:

\begin{quote}
    ``A lot of the time in certain areas like statistics or linear algebra, it gives incorrect answers. It’s obviously incorrect.'' – Focus Group 1, Speaker 3

    ``It would make it look really, really right if you lack the sense of judgment… I couldn’t tell whether you’re accurate or not.'' – Focus Group 5, Speaker 4

    ``You just told me... I cannot access articles through links. It was just funny how... really obvious things beyond its capability.'' – Focus Group 6, Speaker 2
\end{quote}

%
%
%
\subsubsection{RQ2: Are Students Open to Using GenAI in Their Learning? If So, In What Scenarios?}

%
%
%
\paragraph{AI as a Support Tool for Learning}
While some students raised concerns about over-reliance on GenAI, many others described it as a valuable resource to scaffold understanding and clarify unfamiliar concepts rather than using it as a replacement tool. When used as a support tool rather than a solution, GenAI was well-received by many students who saw it as an opportunity to support their thinking:

\begin{quote}
    ``I basically use it to break down hard concepts into simpler terms.'' – Focus Group 6, Speaker 3

    ``It's sort of like questions I'd ask a TA. I just ask Chat first.'' – Focus Group 3, Speaker 5

    ``I primarily use it for research… it’s been really good for taking notes.'' – Focus Group 5, Speaker 2

    ``It becomes useful in your programming... I’ll ask, what’s an example code using this documentation?'' – Focus Group 3, Speaker 4
\end{quote}

These examples reflect how students leveraged GenAI as an academic resource that complements their learning rather than replacement. They reported using the tool to clarify difficult concepts, brainstorm ideas, or test understanding, which served as an accessible learning aid. Interestingly, students emphasized that GenAI was most effective when used as a starting point, but they still needed to review or analyze generated responses.

%
%
%
\paragraph{Avoiding AI: How Students Set Limits with GenAI}
Even as students embraced GenAI as a learning tool, they also identified clear boundaries for its use. They reported avoiding it in math-heavy subjects, personal essays, or high-stakes assignments due to accuracy concerns or fear of detection. Many chose to rely on their own understanding in these situations. Despite the clear advantages they saw in AI, students expressed clear boundaries and opted not to use GenAI in scenarios where accuracy or integrity were at stake:

\begin{quote}
    ``It’s not super great at math, so any kind of probability stuff, linear algebra, calculus... not too great at.'' – Focus Group 1, Speaker 2

    ``If your code is very complex, sometimes it just doesn’t know where to start.'' –Focus Group 5, Speaker 2

    ``Probably on projects or homework... there’s a risk of an AI detection being run.'' – Focus Group 1, Speaker 3

    ``It just sounds awkward and robotic.'' – Focus Group 5, Speaker 3
\end{quote}

Despite recognizing GenAI's benefits, students quickly identified contexts where its use seemed inappropriate or misguiding. For instance, concerns about precision, creativity, and authenticity led some students to avoid GenAI when performing specific tasks. These insights reveal a critical layer of judgment in the construction of their digital literacy-knowing not just how to use AI, but when not to.

%
%
%
\paragraph{Ethical Concerns and Internal Tension}
Participants frequently used GenAI to support their thinking when facing programming challenges or seeking clarity. However, they often reported deep concerns, especially to fairness, academic integrity, and personal identity. For some, using GenAI raised questions about what it meant to genuinely learn or demonstrate their own thinking:

\begin{quote}
    ``I don’t want it to just generate content for me. I want to make sure I’m still learning.'' \\ - Focus Group 1, Speaker 3

    ``Some professors let us use it if we document everything. Others just say no AI at all.'' \\ - Focus Group 5, Speaker 2

    ``I reduced using it... I just feel like I'm getting more dumb… a puppet of ChatGPT.'' \\ - Focus Group 5, Speaker 4
\end{quote}

This final theme reveals student reflections about personal responsibility, academic integrity, and unclear academic policies. This suggests that students are thinking and concerned about how to ethically use GenAI, but they are often struggling to navigate unclear expectations.  As an ending note, it is worth mentioning that comparative analysis between DSA-CS and DSA-DSAI showed no distinguishable differences in the qualitative data.

When examining these themes together, it becomes evident that while the frequency of GenAI use varied among students, the AI Lab Intervention significantly influenced how they interacted with these tools. Over time, students became more intentional in approaching and using AI. In particular, they developed better prompts, gained an understanding of its limitations, and developed a clearer sense of when and how to use GenAI responsibly and effectively. These shifts in their approach open up important questions about how GenAI can be intentionally integrated into STEM undergraduate courses, offering valuable insights for educators and institutions moving forward.


