\section{Discussion}\label{sec:discussion}
%
%
%
%
%
%
%
%
%
%
%
%
%
%
%
%
%
%
%
%
%


%
This study examined the impact of a GenAI-focused instructional intervention on undergraduate students' understanding, perceptions, and usage of GenAI tools across computing and engineering courses. This was the first time testing the AI Lab framework with actual students across different classes and semesters, using survey and interview data. Although the intervention was built on a strong theory, this study provides the first validation of the framework from an empirical perspective.

In analyzing the survey data, we discovered significant changes across multiple measures of students’ comfort and openness to GenAI, with Rank-Biserial Correlations indicating \textbf{large effect sizes} in several key areas. Rather than inducing a uniform shift in overall usage, our results suggest that the AI-Lab fostered more focused and deliberate attitudes toward GenAI. This aligns with the intervention’s dual emphasis: enabling students to experience potential failures of GenAI firsthand while also offering best practices for using the tools responsibly. The strong effect sizes highlight that this approach \emph{did} meaningfully shape student perspectives, especially regarding \emph{why} and \emph{how} to use GenAI, without prompting a dramatic spike in frequency of use. In other words, the framework appears to have succeeded in promoting mindful experimentation and reflective practice, rather than rote adoption.

In particular, the large effect sizes for comfort and openness to GenAI indicate that many students underwent a meaningful shift in attitude, even though frequency of use for homework or programming projects did not significantly increase. This partially dispels the concern that explicit AI instruction would inevitably encourage more academic dishonesty or superficial reliance on GenAI. Rather, students \emph{did} become more willing and  prepared to integrate GenAI into their learning process, but they remained cautious about substituting it for their own problem solving. The absence of major changes in usage frequency thus suggests that students retained healthy skepticism or simply found a comfortable equilibrium in line with existing course policies. Coupled with the qualitative data, these large effect sizes for comfort and openness strongly support our interpretation that the AI-Lab intervention primarily enhanced students’ understanding and critical use of GenAI, rather than pushing them toward the extremes of overreliance.

%
Qualitatively, students described significant improvement in their use approach, learning to prompt GenAI tools more effectively by being more strategic and applying critical thinking when evaluating AI outputs. While most students reported no major increase in usage frequency, they consistently indicated that they became more intentional in their approach. Many also shared that the intervention helped clarify when and how GenAI is most useful and when it is not.

Notably, the intervention increased students' awareness about overreliance, with several expressing concerns about allowing AI to replace their critical thinking. These reflections were not influenced by pressure from the intervention or researchers since the study was educational and exploratory. This approach encouraged students to experiment and reflect organically, leading to failure and discussion. This level of self-reflection is a meaningful outcome since it is difficult to measure through survey instruments alone but surfaced in the qualitative data.
Students also indicated a strong willingness to use GenAI for future academic work, especially with summarization, brainstorming, and structuring.
Importantly, our data does not suggest that teaching students about GenAI encourages academic dishonesty.





%
%
%
\subsection{Differences in Courses} \label{sec:discussion:diff_in_courses}
In addition to second-year computer science classes, we also performed the AI-Lab intervention in a first-year engineering course ENGR, previously introduced in \Cref{sec:results:student_populations}.  The analysis of ENGR showed a higher intervention impact than any other course by number of questions with significant p-values, when bucketed by course over both S24 and F24, as seen in \Cref{tab:number_of_significant_pvalues}.  To further explore the impact of ENGR, one can observe in \Cref{tab:pvalues_f24_comparison} that by adding ENGR to the CS courses in F24, we gain statistical significance in comfort using GenAI for homework problems. This makes intuitive sense because on that question, ENGR had a p-value of 0.0219, CP had 0.0018, and DSA-CS had 0.2761. With the combined CP and DSA-CS p-value of 0.0592 being nearly significant, ENGR pushed it over the edge.  Sample sizes for this data can be found in Tables \ref{tab:combined_perception_semester_counts} and \ref{tab:combined_usage_semester_counts}.

In short, when the AI-Lab was applied to a non-CS discipline, we found that it had an even higher impact than it did in CS, strongly supporting the generalizability of the AI-Lab intervention's positive results.  Anecdotally, this follows from the notion that computer science undergrads tend to be more willing to experiment with and use cutting-edge technologies than other disciplines.  Extending to engineering students provides a structured way for those students to experiment with new technology, that they may not have known how to begin using, in an educationally positive way, potentially leading to even higher educational outcomes for those students.

