One of the biggest differences between semesters that the authors were able to spot was in the question \textit{``How frequently do you use GenAI to get help with homework problems''} from the pre- and post-usage surveys.  We present the pre-survey results in \Cref{fig:frequency_homework_comparingFallAndSpring}, and note that for F24 the post-survey results virtually did not change and for S24 visually moved 6\% from \textit{Very frequently} to \textit{On occasion}, so the stark disjointedness remains between the two semesters for both pre- and post-surveys.  Recall that populations were primarily composed of DSA-CS students in both semesters (\cref{tab:combined_usage_semester_counts}.  Overall, this indicates two primary things.  First, students in F24 were, on average, much more conservative with their use of GenAI on homework problems than S24 students.  Second, the opinions of F24 students about use on homework problems were much more robust to the AI-Lab intervention than in S24.

Qualitatively, we also observed this difference in focus groups from S24 versus F24.  Note that F24 focus groups are not included in \ref{sec:results:qual_analysis}, but have already concluded and have had preliminary analysis performed.  In S24 focus groups, we observed increased caution and concerns about academic integrity, specifically regarding their frequency of use, due to the intervention.  For example, one student reported, \textit{``The biggest thing is probably just ethics, making sure I’m using it in a responsible way. I don’t want to get an advantage over my peers... I need to find the idea myself.''}  Another said, \textit{``I reduced using it. I just felt like I'm getting more and more stupid and dumb… I just feel like I'm getting more dumb.''}  In F24, we observed that students were more cautious when using GenAI, including more hesitant to use it for homework. They showed concerns about academic integrity and AI detection, as in S24, but it seemed they were trying to use it explicitly as a support tool (which was not observed in the S24 group). A few students in F24 mentioned that some professors had specific rules about it which is likely also a reason to make students more careful.  In S24, the university this study was run at (Purdue University) published recommendations (not requirements) for instructors regarding AI in their syllabus.  By F24, it was announced that all instructors were required to have an AI statement in their syllabus.

\begin{figure}
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=0.5\linewidth]{img/How_frequently_do_you_use_GenAI_to_get_help_with_homework_problems_fall_vs_spring.png}
    \caption{A stark difference between Fall 2024 and Spring 2024, in percentage of students who used GenAI moderately or very frequently compared to never or on occasion.  F24 shows much higher percentages in never or on occasion (52.30\%) compared to S24 (28.49\%).  Note that populations were primarily composed of DSA-CS students in both semesters.  See \Cref{sec:discussion:diff_in_sem} for analysis.}
    \label{fig:frequency_homework_comparingFallAndSpring}
    \Description{A stark difference between Fall 2024 and Spring 2024, in percentage of students who used GenAI moderately or very frequently compared to never or on occasion.  F24 shows much higher percentages in never or on occasion (52.30\%) compared to S24 (28.49\%).  Note that populations were primarily composed of DSA-CS students in both semesters.  See \Cref{sec:discussion:diff_in_sem} for analysis.}
\end{figure}
We conjecture this disjointedness in the two semesters is due to two causes.  First, the purpose of the intervention was to make students more cautious of using the tools, though not to the point of not using them at all.  This leads to little to no change for a group of students who already incorporate nuance and caution when using GenAI tools. Second, F24 undergrads are the first group of students who have had access to models good enough to do their homework for their entire collegiate career (``good enough models'' started when GPT-4 was released on 3/14/23, just before their freshmen year).  This likely has led to learning the hard way that overreliance won't help them pass tests and won't teach them what they're paying to learn.  In this sense, a longer exposure to the tool has led to evolved maturity in students.

At this point, the authors have decidedly descended into pure conjecture.  Future studies with longer-lasting datasets are needed to explicitly peel back the intricacies of the evolution of CS undergraduate student perspectives and usage of GenAI in collegiate settings.
%


%
%
%
\subsection{What We Didn't See (Null Findings)}
At this point we return to data analysis focused on only CS courses.  Although we saw shifts in 6 of the 9 questions, we did not see significant differences in openness to using GenAI to help with debugging as well as how frequently students use GenAI on programming projects and homework problems.

Looking at the plots for debugging openness (\Cref{tab:combined:open:debugging}), it is clear that students were already very open to using GenAI to help debug, and the AI-Lab intervention was not primarily designed to encourage or discourage use of GenAI for debugging.  Comparing to openness of conceptual questions and homework problems, Tables \ref{tab:combined:open:conceptual} and \ref{tab:combined:open:homework}, the intervention was directly designed to help students experience the benefits of using GenAI for conceptual questions and caution them against overreliance, and there was a significant change in those two categories. Furthermore, debugging is very rarely, if ever, explicitly taught in CS collegiate curriculum.  educators expect students to learn it on their own as they progress through the degree, and as a consequence, a student is never graded on such activities.  As a student, it then becomes much less risky and much less taboo to use the new shiny tool on a non-graded, non-taught activity that is a frequent pain point.  It is worth noting that one of the primary ways CS educators implement learning through struggling is through the process of debugging, so this position may need more study.  The frequency of use and comfort in using GenAI for debugging did significantly change, however.

As we did not see any statistically significant change in frequency of use on programming projects and homework problems, we infer that either students were already using these tools with some caution. If there had been students at either extreme, overreliance or zero beneficial usage, they would have both shown up in the focus groups and shown up as moving data points in the statistical analysis.

In summary, the unchanged frequency of GenAI use in programming projects and homework suggests that students already exhibit well-established, cautious practices in these contexts. Their behavior, likely shaped by ingrained study habits and the academic stakes involved, appears robust and resistant to intervention.


%
%
%
\subsection{If I teach them GenAI, will they use it to cheat more?}
One frequent concern among faculty is that explicitly teaching students to use GenAI might encourage more academic dishonesty. However, our findings do not support this worry. If the intervention had spurred widespread “cheating” behaviors, we would have expected significant increases in GenAI usage on graded work. Instead, not only did we fail to observe any statistical rise in frequency of use for homework or projects, but the only notable (albeit small) change was actually a slight shift from ``moderate'' or ``very frequent'' usage down to “on occasion.” In other words, rather than pushing students toward reliance on AI for answers, the intervention appears to have encouraged a more restrained and reflective approach, reinforcing that these tools should supplement rather than replace their own intellectual efforts.

%
%
%
\subsection{Conclusions and Future Outlooks}
Overall, our findings reinforce that a carefully designed GenAI intervention -- here, the AI-Lab framework -- can promote more mindful and educationally beneficial usage of AI tools. Students became measurably more comfortable and open to GenAI for conceptual and debugging help, and concerns about it boosting plagiarism were not represented in our data. Instead, they typically used GenAI in moderation, adopting it as a support tool rather than a shortcut. While usage frequency remained relatively stable, students gained deeper insights into GenAI’s limitations and capabilities, suggesting that structured scaffolding successfully encouraged reflective practice. Looking ahead, future work should explore how these effects evolve with larger and more diverse student populations, track and examine semester-to-semester shifts as student perspectives and usage patterns continue to change, and investigate how different instructional contexts or disciplines beyond computer science and engineering might be able to adapt and leverage the AI-Lab approach.





%
%
%
\section{Appendix}
%
%
%
%

\subsection{Focus Group Questionnaire}\label{app:focus_group_questionnaire}
Below is the focus group questionnaire developed to answer the research questions in \Cref{sec:intro:RQs}.  Please refer to \Cref{sec:methods:qual} for details on qualitative methods and \Cref{sec:results:qual_analysis} for details on qualitative analysis.
\begin{enumerate}
    \item How do you define what GenAI is?
    \item What scenarios within the school context do you feel comfortable with using AI?
    \item  What scenarios within the school context are you uncomfortable with using AI?
    \item What was your experience like with the AI lab?
    \item  What are examples of challenges you experienced while using (or attempting to use) Gen AI?
    \item On the survey, some students reported feeling comfortable with GenAI being used on homework or programming projects, while other students reported feeling uncomfortable with GenAI being used on homework or programming projects? - what are your feelings about this? Do you agree or disagree? Why?
    \item Did GenAI help you through your lab? If so, can you provide an example?  If not, what resources did you use?
    \item Before the GenAI lab, how much did you use AI for school related tasks?
    \item After the GenAI lab, did you usage increase or decrease? In what ways?
    \item What motivates you to use GenAI?
    \item Is there anything that discourages your use of Gen AI?
    \item Anything else you’d like to share regarding GenAI usage that you think we should know?
\end{enumerate}



