\section{Conclusion}

This paper introduced a new IOPP protocol designed for codes on graphs. It achieves practical complexity, and soundness competing with used IOPP for Reed-Solomon codes.
Future research will focus on generalizing the cuts to more than two subsets and allowing multiple classes of equivalence of cut-graphs.

However, without arithmetization, it cannot provide new SNARK constructions.
Since our codes are built using Reed-Solomon codes as base codes, it may enable efficient arithmetizations.
Indeed, among the main arithmetization techniques, the PlonK variants \cite{GWZC19} and R1CS \cite{BCRSVW19} write the computation to be verified as an arithmetic circuit in which each gate represents a polynomial.
Then, writing that circuit as a De Bruijn graph \cite{Spi95} makes it regular and probably suitable for our protocol.

