\subsection{The SQCIR Mob propagation model}

A mob is an event managed through social media networks, email, SMS, or other digital communication technologies in which a group of people gathers online or offline to collectively conduct an act and then spread (fast or over time). Analyzing such a phenomenon is challenging due to a lack of data, theoretical methods, and resources. For this purpose, we employ epidemiological techniques to model the behavior of mobbers, utilizing the mobility equation for tracking mobber. The total population is divided into five compartments: $S_{i}(t)$, the number of susceptible individuals who have not encountered the mob; $Q_{i}(t)$, the number of individuals who are initially suspicious or influenced by media, temporally not spreading the mob; $C_{i}(t)$, the Individual who have encountered the mob but are not actively spreading it; $I_{i}(t)$, the Individual who believes and actively spread the mob; $R_{i}(t)$, the Individual who no longer spread the mob, either become they lost interest or encountered reliable information.
 Our model is controlled by the following system of five ordinary differential equations. A transfer diagram of the model is shown in Fig. \ref{SEIREq}, and the model's parameters are given in Table \ref{tab:seiz-parameters}.

