\begin{abstract}

Delta debugging assumes search space monotonicity: if a program causes a failure, any supersets of that program will also induce the same failure, permitting the exclusion of subsets of non-failure-inducing programs. However, this assumption does not always hold in practice. This paper introduces Probabilistic Monotonicity Assessment (\PMA), enhancing the efficiency of \ddmin-style algorithms without sacrificing effectiveness. \PMA dynamically models and assesses the search space's monotonicity based on prior tests tried during the debugging process and uses a confidence function to quantify monotonicity, thereby enabling the probabilistic exclusion of subsets of non-failure-inducing programs. Our approach significantly reduces redundant tests that would otherwise be performed, without compromising the quality of the reduction.

We evaluated \PMA against two leading \ddmin-style tools, \CHISEL and \ProbDD. Our findings indicate that \PMA cuts processing time by 59.2\% compared to \CHISEL, accelerates the reduction process (i.e., the number of tokens deleted per second) by $3.32\times$, and decreases the sizes of the final reduced programs by 6.7\%. Against \ProbDD, \PMA reduces processing time by 22.0\%, achieves a $1.34\times$ speedup in the reduction process, and further decreases the sizes of the final reduced programs by 3.0\%. These findings affirm \PMA's role in significantly improving delta debugging’s efficiency while maintaining or enhancing its effectiveness.
\end{abstract}

\begin{comment}
Delta debugging is a critical technique for minimizing inputs while preserving target properties, widely applied in software engineering for compiler development, regression fault localization, and software debloating. Despite significant advancements, existing approaches often struggle with efficiency and effectiveness, particularly in complex scenarios. Recent trends in delta debugging have employed machine learning and probabilistic methods, such as ProbDD, which focus on updating individual element probabilities but overlook the inherent nature of the search space. 

This paper introduces Probabilistic Monotonicity Assessments (PMA), a novel delta debugging algorithm that dynamically models and assesses the monotonicity of the search space to enhance debugging efficiency. Our key insight is that ddmin algorithms contain substantial redundancy, while probabilistic algorithms often neglect the characteristics of the search space. PMA addresses these limitations by employing a holistic approach, evaluating the likelihood of monotonicity across the entire search space through a confidence function that quantifies the degree of monotonicity and guides the testing strategy in real-time. By strategically skipping unnecessary test sequences, PMA significantly reduces the number of required tests while maintaining reduction quality. 
We implement PMA and evaluate its performance against state-of-the-art approaches across diverse benchmarks. Our results demonstrate that PMA reduces processing time by 57.0\% compared to CHISEL, achieves a 2.485x speedup in reduction speed, and further reduces output sizes by 0.5\%. When compared to ProbDD-enhanced CHISEL, PMA reduces processing time by 26.3\%, attains a 1.392x speedup, and achieves an additional 1.0\% reduction in output sizes, showcasing the effectiveness of our approach in improving delta debugging efficiency and quality.
\end{comment}



